1
00:00:00,640 --> 00:00:02,632
Night. Three hundred thousand years ago.

2
00:00:03,010 --> 00:00:04,791
Fardin is hiding behind a rock.

3
00:00:04,831 --> 00:00:05,970
And he is not alone.

4
00:00:06,176 --> 00:00:07,665
Something is circling the camp.

5
00:00:07,705 --> 00:00:08,955
Then comes the sound.

6
00:00:10,851 --> 00:00:12,006
Spotted hyenas.

7
00:00:12,045 --> 00:00:14,338
And Fardin just realized
something terrible.

8
00:00:14,566 --> 00:00:17,049
The people around this fire can't see him.

9
00:00:17,159 --> 00:00:19,612
But he has no idea
if the hyenas can.

10
00:00:20,977 --> 00:00:24,530
But to understand how he got here,
we need to go back a few hours.

11
00:00:25,347 --> 00:00:26,523
This is Fardin.

12
00:00:26,678 --> 00:00:28,206
Normal guy. Red hoodie.

13
00:00:28,246 --> 00:00:29,346
One very unusual watch.

14
00:00:29,457 --> 00:00:32,465
Turn the dial, and he falls
back through time as a ghost.

15
00:00:32,636 --> 00:00:34,816
Nobody there can see him or hear him.

16
00:00:34,876 --> 00:00:36,036
He can only watch.

17
00:00:36,180 --> 00:00:38,505
Tonight, he set the dial
further back than ever.

18
00:00:39,958 --> 00:00:41,327
Three hundred thousand years.

19
00:00:45,355 --> 00:00:49,613
In the next twenty four hours, he'll see
three things that changed how we think

20
00:00:49,653 --> 00:00:50,807
about our species.

21
00:00:50,974 --> 00:00:52,656
A hunt with stone tipped spears.

22
00:00:52,827 --> 00:00:56,359
A rock trick so clever,
it lasted two hundred thousand years.

23
00:00:56,513 --> 00:01:00,625
And by tonight, the strangest discovery
of all: who else was out there.

24
00:01:01,636 --> 00:01:06,899
Dawn. Fardin is standing on a dry, rocky
hillside, in what will one day be Morocco.

25
00:01:07,099 --> 00:01:11,067
There are no countries yet, no farms,
and no cities anywhere on Earth.

26
00:01:11,286 --> 00:01:13,416
Scientists call this place Jebel Irhoud.

27
00:01:13,601 --> 00:01:19,146
In 2017, researchers announced that human
fossils found here are about three hundred

28
00:01:19,186 --> 00:01:20,610
thousand years old.

29
00:01:20,811 --> 00:01:23,856
They are the oldest known
fossils of our own species.

30
00:01:23,926 --> 00:01:25,071
Homo sapiens.

31
00:01:25,784 --> 00:01:26,567
And there they are.

32
00:01:26,607 --> 00:01:28,868
A small group, waking up beside a cave.

33
00:01:29,096 --> 00:01:30,647
Fardin's first thought?

34
00:01:30,687 --> 00:01:31,977
They look... surprisingly normal.

35
00:01:32,325 --> 00:01:34,343
And he's right, mostly.

36
00:01:34,389 --> 00:01:35,965
Their faces were a lot like ours.

37
00:01:36,005 --> 00:01:38,483
But the back of the skull
was longer and lower.

38
00:01:38,704 --> 00:01:42,187
Their brains were about as big
as ours, but shaped differently.

39
00:01:42,347 --> 00:01:45,877
The rounder brain shape we have
today seems to have appeared later.

40
00:01:46,645 --> 00:01:48,812
Here's a way to feel
how long ago this is.

41
00:01:48,992 --> 00:01:53,301
Squeeze the whole story of our
species into one twenty four hour day.

42
00:01:53,511 --> 00:01:56,572
Farming doesn't show up
until about eleven PM.

43
00:01:56,767 --> 00:01:57,588
The pyramids?

44
00:01:57,655 --> 00:01:58,810
Eleven thirty eight.

45
00:01:58,850 --> 00:02:02,671
Almost everything in your history
books happens in the last half hour.

46
00:02:03,415 --> 00:02:05,225
And forget the Sahara you know.

47
00:02:05,315 --> 00:02:09,739
At times, North Africa was much greener,
with grasslands, lakes and rivers.

48
00:02:09,904 --> 00:02:12,836
Gazelles and other grazing
animals roamed here.

49
00:02:12,976 --> 00:02:16,058
Fardin tries waving at a
man walking right past him.

50
00:02:17,416 --> 00:02:18,737
Nothing. Not even a blink.

51
00:02:18,777 --> 00:02:20,119
He really is a ghost here.

52
00:02:20,315 --> 00:02:21,250
But that's fine.

53
00:02:21,317 --> 00:02:23,233
Because the hunt is about to begin.

54
00:02:23,283 --> 00:02:26,112
And it's nothing compared
to what happens after dark.

55
00:02:26,953 --> 00:02:27,711
Mid morning.

56
00:02:27,751 --> 00:02:32,383
A few hunters grab their spears and head
out, and Fardin tags along, three steps

57
00:02:32,423 --> 00:02:38,294
behind. Archaeologists dug up many animal
bones at Jebel Irhoud, and the most common

58
00:02:38,334 --> 00:02:40,126
by far belonged to gazelles.

59
00:02:40,316 --> 00:02:42,808
Many carry cut marks from stone tools.

60
00:02:43,557 --> 00:02:44,672
Here's the problem.

61
00:02:44,754 --> 00:02:48,192
A gazelle can sprint at up to
around eighty kilometers an hour.

62
00:02:48,302 --> 00:02:49,923
No human can catch one.

63
00:02:50,137 --> 00:02:51,669
So the hunters creep in low.

64
00:02:51,709 --> 00:02:52,742
Quiet. Downwind.

65
00:02:52,782 --> 00:02:54,371
Closer. And closer.

66
00:02:55,572 --> 00:02:56,292
Now!

67
00:02:57,018 --> 00:02:59,142
The spear flies, and misses.

68
00:02:59,182 --> 00:03:00,320
The whole herd explodes away.

69
00:03:00,564 --> 00:03:03,879
Fardin is laughing so hard
he forgets he's invisible.

70
00:03:04,009 --> 00:03:06,524
Then he remembers, and laughs even louder.

71
00:03:08,123 --> 00:03:09,461
But the hunters don't give up.

72
00:03:09,501 --> 00:03:10,501
They spread out.

73
00:03:10,581 --> 00:03:12,289
Patient. Working as a team.

74
00:03:12,352 --> 00:03:14,716
Hunting like this takes
planning, and trust.

75
00:03:14,897 --> 00:03:17,342
On the next try,
one throw finds its mark.

76
00:03:17,889 --> 00:03:22,187
Some stone points from this site look like
they were made to be fixed onto wooden

77
00:03:22,227 --> 00:03:26,199
shafts. Did these hunters throw
their spears, or stab up close?

78
00:03:26,239 --> 00:03:28,232
Scientists are still debating that.

79
00:03:28,461 --> 00:03:31,158
And the strangest part of
their day hasn't happened yet.

80
00:03:31,988 --> 00:03:33,650
Back at camp, it's lunchtime.

81
00:03:33,690 --> 00:03:35,545
And Fardin learns a rule fast.

82
00:03:35,646 --> 00:03:36,924
Nothing gets wasted.

83
00:03:37,099 --> 00:03:40,963
They slice the meat with stone flakes,
sharp enough to cut through hide.

84
00:03:41,697 --> 00:03:46,378
Then someone grabs a heavy rock, places a
leg bone on a flat stone, and smashes

85
00:03:46,418 --> 00:03:48,308
it open. Inside is marrow.

86
00:03:48,380 --> 00:03:50,333
Soft, fatty, and full of energy.

87
00:03:50,373 --> 00:03:51,963
They scoop it out, and eat it.

88
00:03:52,003 --> 00:03:54,485
Raw. Fardin looks like
he's about to be sick.

89
00:03:56,138 --> 00:03:57,946
But to them, this is a treat.

90
00:03:58,066 --> 00:04:02,694
Humans and our ancestors had been cracking
bones for marrow for more than a million

91
00:04:02,734 --> 00:04:03,454
years.

92
00:04:03,973 --> 00:04:05,176
Did they cook any of it?

93
00:04:05,216 --> 00:04:09,630
Possibly. Some flint tools here were
burned in fire, and that's actually how

94
00:04:09,670 --> 00:04:11,203
scientists dated the site.

95
00:04:11,393 --> 00:04:15,300
But how often people cooked their
food this long ago is still uncertain.

96
00:04:15,571 --> 00:04:17,868
They almost certainly ate plants too.

97
00:04:17,908 --> 00:04:19,868
Roots, seeds and fruits.

98
00:04:19,978 --> 00:04:23,729
But plants rarely survive this long,
so most of the menu is lost.

99
00:04:23,911 --> 00:04:25,525
Fardin decides to skip lunch.

100
00:04:25,736 --> 00:04:29,704
Good call. Because later tonight, he'll
find out these people were not alone on

101
00:04:29,744 --> 00:04:30,512
this planet.

102
00:04:31,330 --> 00:04:35,792
Afternoon. In the shade of the cliff,
an older man sits down with two stones.

103
00:04:35,962 --> 00:04:37,724
Fardin sits right next to him.

104
00:04:37,918 --> 00:04:40,028
This, it turns out, is high technology.

105
00:04:40,241 --> 00:04:41,956
He doesn't just smash the rock.

106
00:04:42,136 --> 00:04:46,036
He shapes it, chip by chip,
as if he's planning several moves ahead.

107
00:04:46,076 --> 00:04:48,852
Every strike has to land
at just the right angle.

108
00:04:49,606 --> 00:04:51,096
Then, one precise strike...

109
00:04:52,068 --> 00:04:55,240
and a ready made blade pops off,
sharp along its edges.

110
00:04:55,398 --> 00:04:58,482
Archaeologists call this
the Levallois technique.

111
00:04:58,662 --> 00:05:03,302
The toolmaker had to picture the final
tool inside the stone, before it existed.

112
00:05:03,531 --> 00:05:07,951
Methods like this were used across Africa
and beyond for more than two hundred

113
00:05:07,991 --> 00:05:09,021
thousand years.

114
00:05:09,746 --> 00:05:11,798
Fire is part of life here too.

115
00:05:11,838 --> 00:05:14,758
Burnt flint and bone tell
us these people used it.

116
00:05:14,978 --> 00:05:18,610
Fire meant warmth, light,
protection, and maybe cooked food.

117
00:05:18,827 --> 00:05:23,416
And far away in Kenya, at a site
called Olorgesailie, people from around

118
00:05:23,456 --> 00:05:28,416
this same time carried black obsidian rock
from as far as ninety kilometers away.

119
00:05:28,651 --> 00:05:31,992
Ninety kilometers is roughly
two marathons, on foot.

120
00:05:32,122 --> 00:05:34,742
They also collected
red and black pigments.

121
00:05:34,842 --> 00:05:36,392
Was that trade between groups?

122
00:05:36,432 --> 00:05:38,032
Some scientists think so.

123
00:05:38,072 --> 00:05:39,248
Others aren't sure.

124
00:05:40,005 --> 00:05:43,822
Either way, these were people who
planned ahead, remembered and connected.

125
00:05:43,932 --> 00:05:45,681
Just with very different gear.

126
00:05:45,894 --> 00:05:47,574
Then the sun starts to sink.

127
00:05:47,714 --> 00:05:52,079
And Fardin is about to find out why
nobody here wanders off after dark.

128
00:05:52,938 --> 00:05:55,700
Sunset. The group pulls
in close around the fire.

129
00:05:55,810 --> 00:05:57,592
Fardin finally relaxes.

130
00:05:57,754 --> 00:05:58,649
Big mistake.

131
00:05:59,940 --> 00:06:01,639
Out in the dark, shapes are moving.

132
00:06:01,835 --> 00:06:06,482
Across Africa, early humans shared the
land with lions, leopards and spotted

133
00:06:06,522 --> 00:06:08,158
hyenas. And here we are.

134
00:06:08,198 --> 00:06:10,000
This is the moment from the beginning.

135
00:06:10,735 --> 00:06:12,315
The hyenas circle closer.

136
00:06:12,355 --> 00:06:14,286
The adults rise, spears ready.

137
00:06:14,494 --> 00:06:17,683
Someone grabs a burning branch,
and hurls it toward the dark.

138
00:06:18,130 --> 00:06:19,251
The eyes back away.

139
00:06:19,632 --> 00:06:22,546
Fire probably helped keep
predators at a distance.

140
00:06:22,636 --> 00:06:25,860
It's one of the oldest safety
tricks our species has.

141
00:06:26,617 --> 00:06:28,303
Fardin's heart is pounding.

142
00:06:28,393 --> 00:06:32,773
Then he remembers, he's the only one here
who can literally walk through a wall.

143
00:06:32,967 --> 00:06:34,525
He's still not going out there.

144
00:06:36,319 --> 00:06:40,348
Late at night, inside the cave,
people lie down on beds of grass.

145
00:06:40,448 --> 00:06:41,608
Fardin tries one.

146
00:06:41,648 --> 00:06:43,177
Surprisingly comfy.

147
00:06:43,391 --> 00:06:44,803
This part isn't a guess.

148
00:06:44,873 --> 00:06:49,607
At Border Cave in South Africa, scientists
found grass bedding about two hundred

149
00:06:49,647 --> 00:06:50,940
thousand years old.

150
00:06:51,129 --> 00:06:54,942
It was laid on top of ash,
possibly to keep crawling insects away.

151
00:06:55,704 --> 00:06:58,497
Fardin lies awake,
staring out at the stars.

152
00:06:58,547 --> 00:07:00,977
Then his watch buzzes, with one last fact.

153
00:07:01,017 --> 00:07:02,110
The biggest one.

154
00:07:02,470 --> 00:07:04,982
These people were not
the only humans on Earth.

155
00:07:06,592 --> 00:07:11,183
Around this time, there were Neanderthal
ancestors in Europe, and mysterious

156
00:07:11,223 --> 00:07:14,511
Denisovans in Asia,
known mostly from their DNA.

157
00:07:14,735 --> 00:07:19,336
A small brained species called
Homo naledi lived in South Africa.

158
00:07:19,556 --> 00:07:23,146
Another kind of archaic human
lived in what is now Zambia.

159
00:07:23,246 --> 00:07:27,406
And on the island of Java,
probably some of the last Homo erectus.

160
00:07:27,559 --> 00:07:31,299
At least five different kinds of humans
may have shared the planet at the same

161
00:07:31,339 --> 00:07:32,059
time.

162
00:07:32,514 --> 00:07:33,592
Today, only one is left.

163
00:07:33,632 --> 00:07:37,156
Us. Scientists are still working out why.

164
00:07:37,306 --> 00:07:41,746
Much later, some of those other humans
even had children with our ancestors.

165
00:07:41,846 --> 00:07:46,932
That's why many people alive today carry
a little Neanderthal or Denisovan DNA.

166
00:07:47,827 --> 00:07:48,849
Dawn is coming.

167
00:07:48,948 --> 00:07:51,314
Fardin's twenty four hours are almost up.

168
00:07:51,494 --> 00:07:53,001
His watch starts to glow.

169
00:07:53,190 --> 00:07:54,922
But the dial is spinning the wrong way.

170
00:07:55,379 --> 00:08:00,258
Next time, Fardin lands in ancient Egypt,
right in the middle of building the Great

171
00:08:00,298 --> 00:08:01,018
Pyramid.

172
00:08:01,632 --> 00:08:04,130
Subscribe, or Fardin stays lost in time.

